<USER_REQUEST>
What type of carbonyl compound is primarily produced when Grignard reagents react with carbon dioxide (CO₂)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The type of carbonyl compound produced is $\boxed{\text{Carboxylic acid}}$.  

**Explanation:**  
1. The Grignaid reagent acts as a nucleophile, attacking the carbonyl oxygen of CO₂.  
2. This forms a enenolate intermediate.  
3. The enenolate is then protonated by water (from the aqueous work-up) to form the corresponding carboxylic acid.  

Thus, the reaction converts CO₂ into a carboxylic acid with two additional carbon atoms added from the Grignaid reagent.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

