The bond between nitrogen (N) and oxygen (O) in the molecule NO2 is polar. This is because oxygen is more electronegized than nitrogen, meaning it has a stronger attraction to electrons in the bond. This results in the oxygen atoms having a partial negative charge (δ⁻) and the nitrogen atom having a partial positive charge (δ⁺). The bond is not fully polarized (like in a full dipole), as NO2 is a linear molecule with two oxygen atoms, leading to resonance and partial delocalization of charge. $\boxed{\text{Polar}}$
