   - Temperature plays a critical role in reaction kinetics, selectivity, and product properties.
   - Higher temperatures generally increase reaction rates, leading to higher reaction rates and shorter reaction times. However, excessively high temperatures can lead to side reactions, decomposition, or the formation of byproducts, reducing yield and yield.
   - For example, in the hydrolysis of fatty acids to form fatty acids, higher temperatures (e.g., 100–180°C) can accelerate the reaction but may also cause hydrolysis of the surfactant or degradation of the surfactant.
