The rate-determining step is the slowest step in a reaction mechanism, which is given as Step III:
\[ \text{COCl} + \text{Cl}_2 \rightarrow \text{COCl}_2 + \text{Cl} \]
The mechanism involves the reaction of \( \text{COCl} \) and \( \text{Cl}_2 \) to form \( \text{COCl}_2 \) and \( \text{Cl} \).
The mechanism involves the reaction of two molecules: \(\text{COCl}\) and \(\text{Cl}_2\).
The molecularity of a reaction is the number of molecules reacting in the elementary step.
Since the rate-determining step involves two molecules, the mechanism is bimolecular.

Thus, the molecularity of the rate-determining step (Step III) is \(\boxed{\text{bimolecular}}\).
