</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The decay length (\(L_{\infty}\)) of an evanescent wave is given by the inverse of the slope of the evanescent wave's slope in the \(x\)-plane, which is given by:

\[ L_{\infty} = \frac{\lambda}{\Delta n} \]

where:
- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the evanescent wave.
- \(\Delta n\) is the difference between the refractive indices of the core and the cladding.

In this case, the core is GaAs and the cladding is Ga\(_{02}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)Al. The difference \(\Delta n = n_{\text{eff}} - n_f = 0.3\) is given, where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the refractive index of the cladding and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the free medium (e.g., air or air-like medium).

Given:
- \(\lambda = 1 \, \mu\text{m} = 100 \, \mu\text{m} = 1000 \, \mu\text{m} = 10000 \, \mu\text{m}\).
- \(\Delta n = 0.3\)

Substituting these values into the formula:

\[ L_{\infty} = \frac{10000 \, \mu\text{m}}{0.3} = 33,333 \, \mu\text{m} = 32.32 \, \text{cm} \]

Thus, the decay length of the evanescent wave is \(\boxed{32.32 \, \text{cm}}\).
