### 1. Stefan-Boltzmann Law: Total Intensity (or Power)
The Stefan-Boltzmann law describes the total intensity (or power) emitted by a black body, which is the total power emitted per unit unit area.

\[
P_{\text{max}} = \lambda_{\text{max}} \lambda_{\text{max}} \propto \lambda_{\text{max}} \lambda_{\text{max}}
\]
The total intensity is given by:
\[
P = \frac{1}{2} \frac{1}{\lambda_{\text{max}}} \lambda_{\text{max}} \propto \lambda_{\text{max}} \lambda_{\text{max}}
\]

The total intensity (or power) emitted by a black body is given by:
\[
P = \sigma \times A \times T = \lambda_{\text{max}} \lambda_{\text{max}2}
\]
where:
- \( P \) is the total power emitted (in watts).
- \( \sigma = 1.0 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{W m}^{-1} \cdot \text{K}^{-1} \) is the Stefan-Boltzmann constant.
- \( A = 1 \, \text{m}^2 \) is the surface area of the black body (in square meters).
- \( T \) is the absolute temperature of the black body (in Kelvin).

### 2. Wien's Law: Wavelength of Maximum Intensity
Wien's law describes the wavelength at which the maximum intensity occurs.
\[
\lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{\lambda_{\text{max}}}{T}
\]
where:
- \( \lambda_{\text{max}} \) is the wavelength at which the maximum intensity occurs (in nanometers).
- \( T \) is the absolute temperature of the black body (in Kelvin).

