To calculate the work done, use the formula:  
\[ W = F \cdot s \cdot \cos \theta \]  
Where:  
- \( F = 60.0 \, \text{N} \) (applied force),  
- \( s = 10.0 \, \text{m} \) (distance),  
- \( \theta = 60.0° \) (angle between force and direction of motion).  

1. Compute \( \cos 60.0° = 0.5 \).  
2. Plug in the values:  
\[ W = (60.0 \, \text{N}) \cdot (10.0 \, \text{m}) \cdot (0.5) \]  
3. Calculate the work:  
\[ W = 300 \, \text{J} \]  

The work done is \(\boxed{300 \, \text{J}}\).
